cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) is a structural framework for describing economic
  and social change through a common resource-and-capital state K,
  actor-specific owned or attributed resource-and-capital states K_i,
  subjective evaluation and expectation states P_i, actor-side processes and
  events A_i, and realized resource-and-capital changes Delta K. K_i denotes
  ownership, holding, or attribution rather than an actor's subjective access
  or usability belief; actors and observers are not assumed to know K_i
  completely. P_i remains subjective even when similar expectations, norms,
  contracts, or institutional beliefs are widely shared; shared P is therefore
  treated as a collective pattern inferred from actor-specific P rather than an
  objective social-value truth. Contracts and institutions are not folded into
  K merely because they have external or legal existence. Their records and
  events can be observed, their interpretation and expected enforcement can be
  represented in P, and realized resource transfers caused by enforcement are
  represented as Delta K. A single action may produce multiple Delta K records.
  These records can be interpreted as a generalized bookkeeping trace of
  resource and capital transformation. When heterogeneous realized changes are
  mapped through a specified valuation and bookkeeping rule and aggregated
  within an accounting boundary, the final residual can be represented as
  surplus or deficit. Surplus is therefore a derived accounting quantity rather
  than a physical primitive. No separate interval-activity variable is required
  by the Core; gross activity can be reconstructed from action and Delta K event
  histories when needed. Expected realized change E_i[Delta K] is kept distinct
  from preference and planned change. Micro and macro descriptions refer to the
  same common K: actor-level decisions and Delta K records alter ownership and
  capital distributions, while macro projections aggregate the same records
  under broader accounting boundaries. Economic projections can distinguish
  use-value, labor-value measures, exchange value or price, surplus, and capital
  accumulation without imposing a political or normative conclusion as a Core
  axiom. Above this representation, VFT adopts three management rationality
  axioms for decision-making: resource-realization, activity-flow, and
  P-downside. Their priority and division vary by context, role, institution,
  organizational structure, and time horizon. VFT does not fix a universal
  probability measure, expectation-formation equation, deterministic
  action-selection function, state-transition law, ownership rule, valuation
  rule, surplus equation, market-equilibrium condition, rationality weighting
  rule, or micro-to-macro aggregation law.
